concepts.content:
A perceptron is a simple model for a neuron with $n$ inputs $\{x_i\}_{i=1}^n$ and one output $y$.

(see figure: A simple model for a neuron is the perceptron.)

It maps inputs to outputs according to the following equations:
$$\begin{aligned}
z = f(\mathbf{x}) &= \sum_{i=1}^n w_i x_i + b = \mathbf{w}^\mathsf{T}\mathbf{x} + b &\triangleleft \quad \text{linear layer}\\
    g(z) &= \begin{cases}
    1, &\text{if} \quad z > 0\\
    0,              & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases} &\triangleleft \quad \text{activation function}\\
    y &= g(f(\mathbf{x})) &\triangleleft \quad \text{perceptron}
\end{aligned}$$

In this model, we take a weighted sum of the inputs; if that sum exceeds a threshold (here 0), the neuron fires (outputs a 1). The function $f$ is called a linear layer because it computes an affine function of the inputs (a linear function of $\mathbf{x}$ plus a bias $b$). The function $g$ is the activation function, which determines if the neuron fires.

