concepts.content:
The output layer maps a neural representation—a high-dimensional array of floating point numbers—to a desired output representation. In classification problems, the desired output is a class label, and the most common output operation is the softmax function. In image synthesis problems, the desired output is typically a 3D array with dimensions $N \times M \times 3$, and values in the range $[0,255]$. A sigmoid multiplied by 255 is a typical output transformation for this setting. The equations for these two layers are:

$$
\begin{aligned}
    \textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{out}}}[i] &= 
    \frac{e^{-\textcolor[rgb]{0.2, 0.6, 1.0}{\tau}\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i]}}
         {\sum_{k=1}^K e^{-\textcolor[rgb]{0.2, 0.6, 1.0}{\tau}\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[k]}} 
    & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{softmax}\\
    \textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{out}}}[i] &= 
    255*\texttt{sigmoid}(\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i]) 
    & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \text{common layer for image output problems}
\end{aligned}
$$

In the softmax definition, a temperature parameter $\textcolor[rgb]{0.2, 0.6, 1.0}{\tau}$ is used to scale how peaky, or confident, the predictions are. The output layer serves as the input to the loss function, completing the specification of the deep learning problem. Using these outputs in practice requires translating them into actual pictures or decisions, such as taking the argmax of a softmax distribution for classification or rounding values to integers for image formats.

